Root cause:
The bug was caused by the LED control logic not properly following the netdev LED trigger semantics, leading to counter-intuitive blinking behavior when multiple LEDs were used to indicate different link speeds. Specifically, the logic did not account for the link status when determining whether to blink the LEDs, resulting in inverted blinking on LEDs that were not active for a specific speed.

Code statements information:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
if ((on & (MTK_PHY_LED_ON_LINK1000 | MTK_PHY_LED_ON_LINK100 | MTK_PHY_LED_ON_LINK10)) ||
    (blink & (MTK_PHY_LED_BLINK_1000RX | MTK_PHY_LED_BLINK_100RX |
              MTK_PHY_LED_BLINK_10RX | MTK_PHY_LED_BLINK_1000TX |
              MTK_PHY_LED_BLINK_100TX | MTK_PHY_LED_BLINK_10TX)))
```
```c
if (on & (MTK_PHY_LED_ON_LINK1000 | MTK_PHY_LED_ON_LINK100 | MTK_PHY_LED_ON_LINK10))
```
Reason:
The LED control logic did not properly account for the link status when determining whether to blink the LEDs, leading to counter-intuitive blinking behavior when multiple LEDs were used to indicate different link speeds. The conditions checked for specific link speeds without considering the overall link status, resulting in inverted blinking on inactive LEDs.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
if ((on & (MTK_PHY_LED_ON_LINK | MTK_PHY_LED_ON_FDX | MTK_PHY_LED_ON_HDX |
           MTK_PHY_LED_ON_LINKDOWN)) ||
    (blink & (MTK_PHY_LED_BLINK_RX | MTK_PHY_LED_BLINK_TX)))
```
```c
if (on & MTK_PHY_LED_ON_LINK)
```
Reason:
The updated logic consolidates the checks for link status and ensures that blinking behavior is only activated when the link is up, following the netdev LED trigger semantics. This prevents inverted blinking on LEDs that are not active for a specific speed, making the behavior more intuitive and consistent with expected functionality.


